<p>The U.S. military says it struck three small boats it suspected of drug smuggling in Latin American waters on Monday, killing 11 people in the Caribbean and the eastern Pacific. U.S. Southern Command called those targeted “narcoterrorists,” a label several outlets say echoes the Trump administration’s framing of its campaign against maritime trafficking routes. Multiple publications say the strikes push reported fatalities from the months-long effort to at least 145 since early September. Supporters describe the operations as an attempt to disrupt dangerous trafficking networks at sea, while critics—and some coverage—focus on the human toll.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/YtgiiorZ2AWjbXeKrF_T9AI_ul3pcq3z03xHodA5hgpGa-IJQOZbr9XbAEEyuKViDh1ko6zj-DNoYqYuzKOKfpJb0yv3A5hg3jSbZgor0lsrStY2ieVCiKLhAGg9rCn_kvALlcVlgg8Lp2rrvVeO8KOw8sXbZ9EOf1j54fcuwI2kWTf7VnZKf2Rm3GzJoVCwH-GhKrYHsC4qn4UkrDLrBdm-pR3QkhZZh8NETVQ' alt='A U.S. fighter jet flies over international waters in the Caribbean.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>How notable this round was: Several outlets described Monday’s attacks as among the deadliest single-day episodes of the months-long campaign, with strikes spanning both the Caribbean and the eastern Pacific.</li><li>Carrier departure and broader context: El País said the strikes came after the aircraft carrier Gerald Ford left the Caribbean, where it had operated since October as part of Operation Southern Spear. The outlet linked the mission to U.S. pressure on Venezuela in the run-up to the January 3 capture of President Nicolás Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores.</li><li>Campaign scale: Since early September, the U.S. has carried out at least 42 strikes on alleged trafficking routes, according to AP figures cited by several outlets.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. military (Southern Command): Southern Command said it carried out strikes on boats it accused of drug smuggling, describing those targeted as “narcoterrorists.” (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/18/us-strikes-on-3-more-alleged-drug-boats-kill-11-people-military-says'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>The Guardian / AP framing: The Guardian, citing AP figures, emphasized the accumulating death toll since September and linked the campaign to President Trump’s call for attacks on people deemed “narco-terrorists” aboard small vessels. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/17/us-military-boats-strikes'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>World Socialist Web Site: WSWS described the attacks as murders and portrayed the campaign as an escalating “killing spree” off the Latin American coast. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/18/eofh-f18.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/18/us-strikes-on-3-more-alleged-drug-boats-kill-11-people-military-says'>US strikes on three more alleged drug trafficking boats kill 11 people, military says</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/usa/2026-02-17/the-united-states-kills-11-people-in-three-new-strikes-on-alleged-drugrunning-boats-in-the-caribbean-and-the-pacific.html'>The United States kills 11 people in three new strikes on alleged drug‑running boats in the Caribbean and the Pacific</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/18/eofh-f18.html'>US murders 11 people with airstrikes on boats in both Caribbean and Pacific</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/americas/article/3343855/11-killed-us-strikes-3-more-alleged-drug-boats'>11 killed in US strikes on 3 more alleged drug boats</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/17/us-military-boats-strikes'>US says 11 people killed in latest strikes on alleged drug boats</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/us-says-11-dead-in-strikes-on-alleged-drug-smuggling-boats'>US says 11 dead in strikes on three alleged d